sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock
=============================================

The edition's NVIDIA driver is its build of NVIDIA's open kernel modules,
nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d, with HDMI 3D and HDMI deep colour. Those
modules run Turing cards and newer only. NVIDIA's 580 branch is the last that
runs Maxwell, Pascal and Volta cards (NVIDIA's 615 README lists them in its
table "legacy_580.xx"), and nvidia-legacy-580xx-kernel-dkms builds its kernel
modules for the edition's kernels. This package keeps NVIDIA's driver at that
version while it is installed.

/etc/apt/preferences.d/09sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock is
generated from this package's version when it is built. Its four records, in
order:

1. NVIDIA's and Debian's own NVIDIA kernel module packages
   (nvidia-kernel-open-dkms, nvidia-kernel-dkms, nvidia-open-kernel-dkms) and
   the edition's open modules (nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d): priority -1,
   never installed. nvidia-legacy-580xx-kernel-dkms provides
   nvidia-kernel-dkms at its own version, the version of the carried user
   space, so NVIDIA's user space is satisfied by it.
2. Every NVIDIA driver package at the version the 580 module is built for:
   priority 1005. The set is the one sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock names
   (the packages NVIDIA's own nvidia-driver-pinning package pins), with this
   module in the place of the open one.
3. The same packages at any other version: priority 100.
4. This package itself: priority 1001 from the edition's repository.

sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock (the lock of the open modules, version 615)
stays installed beside it: sparky-stereo-cuda-repo depends on it, and every
image has both. Its file, 10sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock, names the same
packages, and APT reads this file first: "The first specific-form record that
matches an available package version determines the priority of the package
version" (apt_preferences(5)). So while this package is installed, its records
decide every version of those packages, and the 615 lock decides none.
Hardware Setup removes this package before it installs the open modules on a
card that runs them.

NVIDIA's user space for 580 (nvidia-driver, its libraries, nvidia-settings,
libnvcuvid1) is NVIDIA's, as the 615 user space is, and NVIDIA publishes it
for Debian only in its CUDA repository for Debian 12 (its repository for
Debian 13 starts at 590). Debian 14's APT cannot read that repository: its
only key, EB693B3035CD5710E231E123A4B469963BF863CC, is bound with a SHA-1
self-signature, and sqv refuses it ("SHA1 is not considered secure since
2026-02-01"; apt 3.3.3, sqv 1.5.0, measured 2026-11-02). So the 580 user
space comes from the edition's repository, and this package writes no
source for NVIDIA's Debian 12 repository: one would make every apt update
fail. The edition carries NVIDIA's own Debian 12 packages there
(nvidia-legacy-580xx-userspace in the build tree): NVIDIA's files byte for
byte, NVIDIA's signature checked with gpgv when they are built, at this
package's version (580.196.07-1+sparkyN), which this lock's 580.196.07-1*
record keeps at 1005; amd64 and the i386 libraries Steam, Wine and 32-bit
games need. Each package's README.sparky-stereo says what is NVIDIA's. CUDA 13 does not run on these cards (NVIDIA's CUDA 13.0 release notes:
"Removed support for Maxwell, Pascal, and Volta GPUs"); CUDA 12 toolkits do,
with this driver.

To leave the 580 driver, purge both packages
("apt purge nvidia-legacy-580xx-kernel-dkms
sparky-stereo-nvidia-legacy-580xx-driver-lock"); removing without purging
keeps the configuration file, and with it the lock.
